    {
      "id": "p1",
      "text": "It started when a power plant unit failed, setting off a chain reaction. The national grid manager declared a grid emergency for the Visayas region, a move that hit ordinary households directly. Officials were forced to cut power to prevent a total blackout, leaving a massive power shortage of over 950 megawatts. This wasn't a problem for the elite; it was a practical crisis affecting everyone's daily life, from bills to basic comfort.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Everyman",
        "fragments": [
          "why-0",
          "how-1",
          "how-2",
          "result-0"
        ],
        "channel_role": "toned"
      },
      "warnings": [],
      "fit": 0.9
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "text": "A broken generator at a major facility was the immediate trigger. But the real squeeze came from high demand driven by hot weather, pushing the grid to its limit. This combination meant more than 950 megawatts became unavailable, a huge capacity loss. The people making the emergency decision had to act, implementing load shedding—a direct, painful measure that underscored how shared the vulnerability of the system really is for everyday users.",
